40,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings suggest a shift in our human resources. While we have plenty of interest in modelling and code, our phenotyping technicians have vanished, though the glasshouse crew remains strong. This may influence our choice.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"Wow, that's a big difference! I found out that computing and simulation are now free, which is awesome! But animal costs went way up, and shipping is getting pricier, though glasshouses are still subsidized.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that catalogue-style projects tend to have a longer, more lasting impact. Also, projects relying on live breeding colonies seem to have much more frequent schedule overruns than computational ones.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, that matches what I found. Field sites are a disaster—floods and fires wiped out whole seasons. Plus, the spectroscopy suite was dead for eleven weeks. If a project relies on those, it's a gamble.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Minako,,"My findings complement yours. The animal facility is now well-supported with new veterinary cover, but the glasshouses are completely booked for the next two growing cycles. This creates a significant bottleneck for any plant-based research.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It seems we are faced with a delicate balance. We have a strong glasshouse crew, yet no space to work. We have many coders, but no senior analysts to lead them. Which path is most sustainable?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Minako, that's so tricky! We have the people for the glasshouses but no room! But wait, if computing is free and we have lots of coders, maybe a computer-heavy project is the safest bet?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Sakura is right. If breeding colonies and field sites are so risky, and the glasshouses are full, a project focused on data or computation seems much more likely to actually finish.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, exactly. Why bet on a project that'll just get stuck because a machine broke or a field burned down? We need something that doesn't rely on luck or a miracle to get done.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Minako,,"We must also consider the repository. The turnaround for deposits is now very fast, which strongly favors projects that produce large amounts of data for public use. This reduces the risk of delays at the final stage.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Servas,,"The pieces are falling into place. We have a surplus of coding talent and a fast repository, but a shortage of phenotyping experts and a lack of glasshouse space. A data-driven approach seems most prudent.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"I totally agree! It's like the stars are aligning for a computer project! No expensive animal fees, no waiting for glasshouses, and plenty of people to help with the code! It sounds like the smartest way to go!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I also think that's the safest path. If we choose a project that creates a lasting resource, like the catalogue-style ones I read about, it would be a real success.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, now you're talking. Let's avoid the disasters. If we pick something that's mostly computation and data, we don't have to worry about broken equipment or flooded fields ruining the whole grant.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Minako,,"The evidence is consistent. We have a bottleneck in glasshouse capacity and a deficit in phenotyping staff, but an abundance of computational resources and efficient data deposition. A project centered on data and simulation is the most viable.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Servas,,It is heartening to see such consensus. We have weighed the risks of the physical world against the strengths of our digital infrastructure. Let us now see which candidates best fit this sustainable path.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Sakura,,"I'm so ready to see the candidates! I bet there's one that fits our ""computer-perfect"" plan exactly! I can't wait to find out who they are!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,I... I hope there is a candidate that combines a lasting resource with a computational focus. That would be the most reliable way to ensure the grant's success.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"Let's just get to the list. If there's a project that doesn't need a field site or a fancy machine to work, that's the one I'm voting for. No more gambling.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Minako,,I agree. We have a clear set of criteria based on the current infrastructure and staffing. Let us review the candidates and select the one that minimizes risk and maximizes utility.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
